#{[17592186365818 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=10{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{10{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{10{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{-x}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{{e}^{x}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{x}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$"] [17592186365825 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-{e}^{x}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-x){e}^{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-x{e}^{x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+(B-1)x{e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-1),\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$"] [17592186365838 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is\n$f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=R{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int dx \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =R{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\underbrace{\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186365858 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{vmatrix}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"] [17592186366191 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))j+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =0\\cdot i-0\\cdot j+(5+\\sqrt{2})k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366198 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ x & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ x & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(-2+2)i-(-4+x)j+(8-2x)k \\\\  & =⟨0,4-x,8-2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(4-x)^{2}+(8-2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-8x+{x}^{2}+64-32x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+80 & =20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+60 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2,\\,6.$"] [17592186366212 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\n3x+6y+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=-2y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z+3 & =0 \\\\ -2y-y+2(0)+3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2y=-2$ and $a=⟨-2,1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x+6y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,6,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z+3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,6,3⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =15i-3j-9k \\\\  & =⟨15,-3,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-2,1,0⟩+t⟨5,-1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2+1=-1,$ and $b=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.$"] [17592186366219 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [17592186366794 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true."] [17592186366836 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z & 0 & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)]k \\\\  & =(x-0)i-(y-\\frac{1}{z})j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j-[-i+2z\\,j-x\\,k] \\\\  & =x\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y)j+i-2z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =(x+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{z}-y-2z)j+x\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,2,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-G))(0,2,1) & =(0+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}-2-2(1))j+0\\,k \\\\  & =i-3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366843 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ -3 & z & 8\\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8\\sqrt{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8\\sqrt{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3)]k \\\\  & =(0-1)i-(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =-i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,j+[-i-j] \\\\  & =-i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,j-i-j \\\\  & =-2\\,i-(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+1)j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(4,3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F+G))(4,3,-1) & =-2\\,i-(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4}}+1)j \\\\  & =-2\\,i-3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366864 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ y & x & -1 \\\\ yz & 0 & -{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[x(-{z}^{2})-(-1)(0)]i-[y(-{z}^{2})-(-1)(yz)]j+[y(0)-x(yz)]k \\\\  & =-x{z}^{2}\\,i+(y{z}^{2}-yz)\\,j-xyz\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-x{z}^{2},y{z}^{2}-yz,-xyz⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(xy-z)⟨0,y,-z⟩+⟨-x{z}^{2},y{z}^{2}-yz,-xyz⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,x{y}^{2}-yz,{z}^{2}-xyz⟩+⟨-x{z}^{2},y{z}^{2}-yz,-xyz⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-x{z}^{2},x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-2yz,{z}^{2}-2xyz⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,1) & =⟨-(0)(1)^{2},0(-1)^{2}+(-1)(1)^{2}-2(-1)(1),(1)^{2}-2(0)(-1)(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367326 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 15 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186367327 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 15 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 18 & 0 & -9 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186367328 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -9 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186367329 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -9 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186367330 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 15 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186367394 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F+G) & =∇\\times (2G)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =2∇\\times F+∇\\times G\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & z & yz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\sqrt{y})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{y})]k \\\\  & =(z-1)i-(0-0)j+(0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}})k \\\\  & =(z-1)\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F+G) & =2∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =2[(z-1)\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,k]+x\\,i-y\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =(x+2z-2)\\,i-y\\,j+(1-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y}})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,1,4),$ we get\n\n$$\n(∇\\times (2F+G))(-1,1,4)=5\\,i-\\,j-\\,k\n$$"] [17592186367401 :question/problem "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}3q & -6 \\\\ 2 & p\\end{vmatrix}=-5.\n$$"] [17592186367401 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have  a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{c|cc}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{array}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3q & -6 \\\\ 2 & p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367408 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=12.\n$$"] [17592186367408 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have  a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{ccc|c}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\hline r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367415 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & ={e}^{-t}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =({e}^{-t}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩)\\times (2{e}^{-t}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)k \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=2{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2t}}{{(\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-t})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2t}}{2\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367881 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$”; “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite”; “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$”."] [17592186367888 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $det(A)>0$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5(11)-2(-4)+4(10) \\\\  & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is positive-definite” is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=11>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local maximum of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$a$ is a local maximum of $f$” only."] [17592186367971 :question/problem "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b \\\\ d & 2\\end{vmatrix}=-7.\n$$"] [17592186367971 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -4\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b \\\\ d & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368113 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368114 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368115 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368116 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368118 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368119 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368120 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368121 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368127 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368128 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368129 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368130 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368491 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368492 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368493 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368494 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368495 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186368541 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(H) & ={h}_{11}{C}_{11}+{h}_{12}{C}_{12}+{h}_{13}{C}_{13}+{h}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 7 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix})-(7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =2(0)-(7(-10)) \\\\  & =-(-70) \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368615 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and $‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩=⟨2,\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨2,\\,\\sqrt{2},0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\sqrt{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368655 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+3))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\sqrt{0}\\,j+({0}^{2}+3)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+0\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,k \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1))\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t-2)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)\\,i+1\\,j+(0-2)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+1\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second component of ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)$ is a special limit.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,k)\\times (j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3)\\,i-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)\\,j+(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =-3i-4\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369686 :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186369686 :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=-2\\cdot 16=-32.\n$$"] [17592186369693 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186369693 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+1\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369695 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186369696 :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186369697 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186369698 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186369699 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370072 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=3+4{e}^{-x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{-x} & {e}^{6x} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3x} & -{e}^{-x} & 6{e}^{6x} \\\\ 9{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{-x} & 36{e}^{6x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{6x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 36\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[1(-36-6)-1(-108-54)+1(-3+9)] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-42+162+6) \\\\  & =126{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{6x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 6{e}^{6x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6{e}^{5x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(3+4{e}^{-x})(7{e}^{5x})}{126{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\left(\\frac{21{e}^{5x}+28{e}^{4x}}{126{e}^{2x}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3x}+\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}({e}^{3x}+2{e}^{2x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370597 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{3}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-2{e}^{2x})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370604 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-1(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+1({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(1)-1(0)+1(1)] \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{({e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))\\cdot 1}{2{e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370611 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-1(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+1({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(1)-1(0)+1(1)] \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2{e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370926 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186370926 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370928 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370929 :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370930 :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370931 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370932 :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186370933 :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186370933 :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=-2\\cdot 6=-12.\n$$"] [17592186370953 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3))=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(F)=-9.\n$$"] [17592186371077 :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186371077 :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+1\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(16-(-2)) \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-4)-2(2-2) \\\\  & =4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=-3\\cdot (-36)+1\\cdot 4=112.\n$$"] [17592186371527 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ -1 & 0 & x{y}^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[z(x{y}^{3})-y(0)]i-[0(x{y}^{3})-y(-1)]j+[0(0)-z(-1)]k \\\\  & =xz{y}^{3}\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =yz⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4}-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,2) & =⟨4(0)(2)(-1)^{3},-2(-1)^{4}-(-1),2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371714 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(-1-4),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-5,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371728 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(0) & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨(0-0),-(-1-1),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371941 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={z}^{4}+1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=4{z}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+(4{z}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371948 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,e].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ and\n\n$$\nD=\\{(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373421 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate $v(1):$\n\n$$\nv(1)=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector and evaluating it at $t=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩, \\\\ a(1) & =⟨-2,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v(1)\\times a(1),||v(1)||,$ and $||v(1)\\times a(1)||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1)\\times a(1) & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-2,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩, \\\\ ||v(1)|| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ ||v(1)\\times a(1)|| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration at $t=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v(1)\\times a(1)||}{||v(1)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal component of acceleration is $\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}.$"] [17592186373428 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =⟨0-0,-(-{cos}^{2}⁡3t-{sin}^{2}⁡3t),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373435 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{5{e}^{2t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t})^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-5{e}^{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}+25{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-5{e}^{2t}-1,-(-10{e}^{2t}-2),2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,2(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373442 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\sqrt{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373778 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )+12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +22 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+22I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+22I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+22{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{22}I-\\frac{1}{22}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{22},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{22},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{22}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{22}\\right)=\\frac{3}{11}.\n$$"] [17592186373965 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2u-4v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,-1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,4,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2u-4v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-4v+u,\\,v+2(2u-4v),\\,u-4v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u-4v,\\,4u-7v,\\,u-4v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-4v,\\,4u-7v,\\,u-4v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-4v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-7v)\\cdot 4+(u-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-6u+8v+16u-28v+u-4v \\\\  & =11u-24v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11u-24v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(11u-24v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373979 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,h(x,z),\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373986 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,4u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=4u-v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-(4u-v),\\,v-(4u-v),\\,4u-v+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u+v,\\,-4u+2v,\\,4u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-2u+v,\\,-4u+2v,\\,4u⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2u+v)\\cdot (-4)+(-4u+2v)\\cdot 1+4u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8u-4v+-4u+2v+4u \\\\  & =8u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8u-2v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(8u-2v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375846 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,3)$ is $p=⟨0,1,3⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =i\\times j \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=k=⟨0,0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩ & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(0)+z(1) & =0(0)+1(0)+3(1) \\\\ 0+0+z & =0+0+3 \\\\ z-3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375853 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375860 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375867 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and the principal normal $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186377076 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 4)\\,-\\,2\\,(2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 1)\\,+\\,1\\,(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0(-12)\\,-\\,2\\,(7)\\,+\\,1\\,(8) \\\\  & =0-14+8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186377228 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=1$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}(i+1\\,j+\\sqrt{2}k)=\\frac{1}{2}(i+j+\\sqrt{2}k) \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+(1-1)k)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point  where $t=1$ is\n\n$$\np=r(1)=6\\,i+2\\,j+3\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j+\\sqrt{2}k)k\\times (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨6,2,3\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|-6-2+6| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186377235 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\ N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨0,3,4⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and the principal normal $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩\\times (-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=(-5)\\cdot (T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=⟨0,4,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ & =⟨0,3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ 0\\cdot x+4\\cdot y+(-3)\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ 4y-3z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186377728 :question/worked-solution "The position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=r(0) & =⟨-2(0),\\,{0}^{2},\\,{0}^{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩ & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+1\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ y+z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378125 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $v\\times a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378132 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $v\\times a,||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,2⟩\\times ⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 2 \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 2 \\\\ -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 2 \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨18\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,-27{cos}^{2}⁡3t-27{sin}^{2}⁡3t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,-27⟩ \\\\  & =9⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,-3⟩, \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡3t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡3t+{sin}^{2}⁡3t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =9\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡3t+4{sin}^{2}⁡3t+9} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡3t+{sin}^{2}⁡3t)+9} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378139 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $v\\times a,||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,0,\\,-6t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 0 & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -6t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2t & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & -6t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-(-12{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}),0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,6{t}^{2},0⟩, \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}(9{t}^{2}+4)} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378146 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(t+1){e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(t+2){e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $v\\times a,||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨(t+1){e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨(t+2){e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ -{e}^{t} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t} & -{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,{e}^{2t}⟩, \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{((t+1){e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-{e}^{t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+2}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{({e}^{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378160 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate $v(0):$\n\n$$\nv(0)=⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector and evaluating it at $t=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩, \\\\ a(0) & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v(0)\\times a(0),||v(0)||,$ and $||v(0)\\times a(0)||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0)\\times a(0) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩, \\\\ ||v(0)|| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ ||v(0)\\times a(0)|| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration at $t=0,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v(0)\\times a(0)||}{||v(0)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal component of acceleration is $1\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}.$"] [17592186378167 :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate $v(1):$\n\n$$\nv(1)=⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector and evaluating it at $t=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t},\\,0,\\,1⟩, \\\\ a(1) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v(1)\\times a(1),||v(1)||,$ and $||v(1)\\times a(1)||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1)\\times a(1) & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ ||v(1)|| & =\\sqrt{0)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}, \\\\ ||v(1)\\times a(1)|| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration at $t=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v(1)\\times a(1)||}{||v(1)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & ≈1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal component of acceleration is approximately \n $1.3\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}.$"] [17592186378566 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 5)-4(8\\cdot k-0\\cdot 5)+6(8\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-32k-80-32k+96 \\\\  & =16-64k \\\\  & =16(1-4k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 16(1-4k) & =0 \\\\ 1-4k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"]}
